# Line endings: LF in the repository AND LF in the working tree.
#
# Until this file existed there was nothing here at all, so every checkout on
# Windows took core.autocrlf=true and wrote CRLF, while anything that rewrote a
# file from a script wrote LF. Git then saw the whole file as changed. A
# 27-line addition to styles.css came out as 10,823 changed lines, and eleven
# files had drifted to CRLF in the repository itself.
#
# `eol=lf` rather than `text=auto` alone is the part that matters: text=auto
# normalises what is STORED but still hands Windows a CRLF working tree, so a
# script's LF output would keep producing the same phantom diff. With eol=lf
# the working tree is LF too, and what a tool writes is what git already has.
* text=auto eol=lf

# tileParser.js holds a literal NUL as a map-key separator — deliberate, and
# explained at `const key`. Git's own heuristic reads that NUL and calls the
# file binary, so until now it refused to diff it and showed "Binary files
# differ" for every change. It is ordinary source; say so.
web/src/lib/tileParser.js text eol=lf diff

# Assets, so neither the heuristic nor the rule above can touch their bytes.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.webp binary
*.svg  text eol=lf
*.ico  binary
*.mp3  binary
*.wav  binary
*.ogg  binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.pdf  binary
